{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkXkFFx6r2CfvNUgaGMi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013WrNygPFQrshCkmxrTcQUB","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"The line opens with the clef and the three-flat key signature. Next come three Bb3 eighths, then a barline. That half bar is the second half of bar 25 (Bb Bb Bb), carried over from the line before. It can't be the end of bar 23, which ends Bb, rest, Bb. It can't be the end of bar 27 either, because the next bar has a rest in second place.\n\nBar 26: Bb eighth, eighth rest, then four Bb eighths (Bb, rest, Bb Bb Bb Bb). This matches.\n\nBar 27: six Bb eighths in two groups of three. This matches.\n\nBar 28: four Bb eighths, a small rest, then the double-bar repeat sign. After the repeat come two rest-like marks before the barline. This matches \"Bb Bb Bb Bb, rest, rest\" with the repeat inside the bar. The copyist seems to place one rest before the repeat and the rest after it.\n\nBar 29: \"rmf:\" (the edition's [rf]) under six low eighths on the bottom line/space, Eb3. A barline closes the line.\n\nSo the line runs from the end of bar 25 through bar 29. The program left out the leading half of bar 25.","right":false,"insights":"Five of these bars are the same repeated Bb eighths. Only the rest in second place pins the first full bar as bar 26, and that is easy to miss. It also hides the three-eighth fragment from bar 25 at the start of the line. Knowing where the line before ended, or treating a half-length opening group as a carried-over bar, would have settled it right away."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5364,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5364},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":